+# BLOCKED: exact streaming median in O(1) memory
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`test_constant_memory` requires `median()` to compute the **exact** median of a
+single-pass iterable of 200,000 numbers while allocating less than 64 KB of
+peak Python memory. That requirement cannot be satisfied for arbitrary input.
+
+## Why it is impossible
+
+The median is the `n/2`-th order statistic. An exact streaming algorithm for
+order statistics has an Ω(n) space lower bound (the classic Munro–Paterson
+result), not O(1). Intuitively, two streams can agree on every value seen so
+far and differ later in a way that changes the median, so a constant-memory
+algorithm cannot distinguish them.
+
+Concretely, for this test the input is 200,000 IEEE-754 doubles (~1.6 MB of raw
+data, several MB as Python floats). Computing the exact middle value means
+retaining enough order information about all of them. Sorting (the current
+implementation) is one way; any other exact method still needs storage linear
+in `n` for a single-pass stream. The 64 KB budget is far below that lower
+bound.
+
+The only ways to "pass" the test would be to:
+
+- spill the data to a temporary file or `mmap` (defeating the O(1)-memory
+  requirement in spirit and merely hiding allocations from `tracemalloc`),
+- exploit the test's specific RNG/input rather than implementing a
+  general-purpose median, or
+- return an approximation, violating the test's exactness requirement.
+
+None of these is a legitimate fix, so none was applied.
+
+## What would unblock it
+
+Pick one of these product requirements:
+
+1. **Approximate median** — use a bounded-memory streaming sketch (e.g.
+   t-digest, Greenwald–Khanna, or a P² estimator) and accept an error bound.
+2. **External/bounded-memory exact median** — allow a documented spill-to-disk
+   or two-pass algorithm, and size the memory test for its fixed buffers.
+3. **Bounded input** — require a list/re-iterable input and allow O(n) memory
+   (or multiple passes with O(1) memory), instead of a single-pass feed.
+
+## What was fixed
+
+The unrelated correctness bug is fixed: `median()` now returns the mean of the
+two middle values for an even count, so `median([1, 2, 3, 4]) == 2.5`.
